### 1.4 Scope and Organization of this thesis

In this thesis, I will present and discuss new measurements of anisotropic flow and its fluctuations, for Au+Au collisions at all BES energies, as well as d+Au, Cu+Cu and Cu+Au at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV, and U+U at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=193\) GeV. The beam energy dependent measurements provide important constraints for the extraction of the specific viscosity \(\eta/s\) (the ratio of shear viscosity to entropy density) as well as its \(T\) and \(\mu_{B}\) dependence. In turn the measurements for different collision systems provide stringent constraints for initial-state models.

The organization of this thesis is as follows:

Chapter 2 gives a brief outline of the RHIC accelerator facility and the detector setup employed for the measurements. In Chapters 3 and 4 the data analysis method is presented with emphasis on the two- and multi-particle correlation techniques [30, 31, 32, 33, 34] employed for the anisotropic flow measurements. The detailed results from these analysis techniques, for all collision systems and beam energies are also presented. In Chapter 5, detailed measurements of the two-, four- and six-particle elliptic flow harmonic, as well as elliptic flow fluctuations are presented and discussed. Chapter 6 summarizes the main findings of this thesis and gives further in-depth discussions on the results obtained. An outlook for the future studies is also presented.

