\[\left\langle\left\langle 2^{\rm d}_{\rm Sub-2}\right\rangle \right\rangle_{\rm n} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rm N}\left(\left({\rm S}^{\rm A}_{1,1}\ {\rm mp}^{\rm B}_{1,1}\right)\ \left\langle 2^{\rm d}_{\rm Sub}\right\rangle_{\rm n}\right)_{\rm i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\rm N }\left({\rm S}^{\rm A}_{1,1}\ {\rm mp}^{\rm B}_{1,1}\right)_{\rm i}}, \tag{3.66}\]

to give the two-particle differential cumulants and flow harmonics as:

\[{\rm C}^{\rm d}_{\rm n}\{2\} = \left\langle\left\langle 2^{\rm d}_{\rm Sub-2}\right\rangle \right\rangle_{\rm n}, \tag{3.67}\] \[{\rm v}^{\rm d}_{\rm n}\{2\} = \frac{{\rm C}^{\rm d}_{\rm n}\{2\}}{\sqrt{{\rm C}_{\rm n}\{2\}}}. \tag{3.68}\]

#### The three sub-events cumulant method

As stated before, multi-particle correlations [34] can suppress the non-flow contributions to the correlations. However, for jets multiple particles can be correlated, which renders the standard cumulant ineffective for suppression of such non-flow contributions. The three sub-events cumulant method was introduced to suppress this non-flow contribution especially for collisions in which jet production is significant.

In this method [34], particles are grouped into three different sub-events (A, B and C) in \(\eta\), with non-overlapping \(\eta\) intervals. In each event, particles are then selected from these sub-events to form correlated multiplets. The introduction of this additional sub-event serves to further reduce the short-range (in \(\eta\)) non-flow correlations usually associated with jets. An additional benefit is that the three sub-events method is also able to reduce the effects of long-range non-flow correlations, i.e. back-to-back di-jet correlations. This is primarily because the particles from the two correlated jets (near- and away-side jets) can only fall into two out of the three sub-events. Thus there will always be at least one particle in the four-particle correlation that is not associated with the di-jet. After averaging all the combinations, the di-jet correlation is significantly suppressed [34]. The di-jet contribution can be further reduced via a small \(\eta\) gaps between the three subevents if the statistical significance of the data allow these additional cuts [34].

For this method, the event-by-event weighted Q vector is evaluated for harmonic n as:

\[{\rm Q}_{\rm A,n,k} = \sum_{\rm i=1}^{\rm M}{\rm w}^{\rm k}_{\rm i}({\rm A}){\rm e}^{{ \rm in}\phi^{\rm A}_{\rm i}}, \tag{3.69}\] \[{\rm S}_{\rm A,p,k} = \left[\sum_{\rm i=1}^{\rm M}{\rm w}^{\rm k}_{\rm i}({\rm A}) \right]^{\rm p}, \tag{3.70}\]

\[{\rm Q}_{\rm B,n,k} = \sum_{\rm i=1}^{\rm M}{\rm w}^{\rm k}_{\rm i}({\rm B}){\rm e}^{{ \rm in}\phi^{\rm B}_{\rm i}}, \tag{3.71}\] \[{\rm S}_{\rm B,p,k} = \left[\sum_{\rm i=1}^{\rm M}{\rm w}^{\rm k}_{\rm i}({\rm B}) \right]^{\rm p}. \tag{3.72}\]\[{\rm Q}_{{\rm C},{\rm n},{\rm k}} = \sum_{{\rm i}=1}^{{\rm M}}{\rm w}_{{\rm i}}^{{\rm k}}({\rm C}){\rm e }^{{\rm i}\rm\rm\rm\rm\rm\rm\rm\Theta_{i}^{C}}, \tag{3.73}\] \[{\rm S}_{{\rm C},{\rm p},{\rm k}} = \left[\sum_{{\rm i}=1}^{{\rm M}}{\rm w}_{{\rm i}}^{{\rm k}}({\rm C })\right]^{{\rm p}}. \tag{3.74}\]

Due to symmetry, there are six ways to construct the 4-particle correlations:

\[\left\langle 4^{Sub-3}\right\rangle = e^{\phi_{A}+\phi_{A}^{*}-\phi_{B}-\phi_{C}}, \tag{3.75}\] \[= e^{\phi_{B}+\phi_{B}^{*}-\phi_{A}-\phi_{C}},\] \[= e^{\phi_{C}+\phi_{C}^{*}-\phi_{A}-\phi_{B}},\] \[= e^{\phi_{A}+\phi_{B}-\phi_{A}^{*}-\phi_{C}},\] \[= e^{\phi_{B}+\phi_{C}-\phi_{B}^{*}-\phi_{A}},\] \[= e^{\phi_{C}+\phi_{A}^{*}-\phi_{C}^{*}-\phi_{B}},\]

where \(\phi_{X}^{*}\) belong to the same sub-event \(X\) but different from \(\phi_{X}\). Further details can be found in Ref.[34].

We will focus only on the first one \(e^{\phi_{A}+\phi_{A}^{*}-\phi_{B}-\phi_{C}}\), which leads to:

\[\left\langle 4^{Sub-3}\right\rangle = (Re(Q_{A,n,1}Q_{b,n,1}^{*}Q_{A,n,1}Q_{C,n,1}^{*}) \tag{3.76}\] \[- Re(Q_{A,2n,2}Q_{B,n,1}^{*}Q_{C,n,1}^{*}))/M_{4}^{Sub-3}.\]

Then,

\[\left\langle\left\langle 4_{{\rm Sub}-3}\right\rangle \right\rangle_{\rm n} = \frac{\sum_{{\rm i}=1}^{{\rm N}}\left({\rm M}_{4}^{{\rm Sub}-3} \left\langle 4_{{\rm Sub}-3}\right\rangle_{\rm n}\right)_{\rm i}}{\sum_{{\rm i}=1}^{{ \rm N}}\left({\rm M}_{4}^{{\rm Sub}-3}\right)_{\rm i}}, \tag{3.77}\]

where n and m are harmonic orders and \({\rm M}_{4}\) is:

\[{\rm M}_{4}^{{\rm Sub}-3} = ({\rm S}_{{\rm A},2,1}-{\rm S}_{{\rm A},1,2})({\rm S}_{{\rm B}, 1,1}{\rm S}_{{\rm C},1,1}) \tag{3.78}\]

This gives the four-particle cumulants as:

\[{\rm C}_{\rm n}^{{\rm Sub}-3}\{4\} = \left\langle\left\langle 4_{{\rm Sub}-3}\right\rangle \right\rangle_{\rm n}-2\left\langle\left\langle 2_{{\rm Sub}-2}\right\rangle \right\rangle_{\rm n}^{{\rm AB}}\left\langle\left\langle 2_{{\rm Sub}-2} \right\rangle\right\rangle_{\rm n}^{{\rm AC}}, \tag{3.79}\]

and the four-particle flow harmonics as:

\[{\rm v}_{\rm n}^{4}\{4\} = {\rm C}_{\rm n}^{{\rm Sub}-3}\{4\} \tag{3.80}\]

#### Flow fluctuations effects

In addition to non-flow, fluctuations also impact \(v_{n}\) experimental measurements. When the heavy-ion collision starts, the nucleon distributions in the overlap region fluctuates event-by-event. These initial geometry fluctuations will drive flow fluctuations in the final state. As discussed earlier the \(2m\)-particle azimuthal correlator is obtained by averaging over all unique combinations in one event then overall events [32, 82]:

\[\langle 2m\rangle = \langle e^{in\sum_{j=1}^{m}(\phi_{2j-1}-\phi_{2j})}\rangle \tag{3.81}\]

where the mean \(\langle...\rangle\) is taken within a single event. Assuming that the non-flow contributions are negligible, we can write:

\[v_{n}\{2\}^{2} = \langle\langle 2\rangle\rangle, \tag{3.82}\] \[v_{n}\{4\}^{4} = 2\langle\langle 2\rangle\rangle-\langle\langle 4\rangle\rangle.\]

Therefore we can relate the ratio \(v_{n}\{4\}/v_{n}\{2\}\) to the variance of \(v_{n}^{2}\), \(\sigma^{2}(v_{n}^{2})\ \equiv\ \langle v_{2}^{4}\rangle-\langle v_{2}^{2}\rangle^{2}\)

\[\Big{(}\frac{v_{n}\{4\}}{v_{n}\{2\}}\Big{)}^{4} = 2-\frac{\langle v_{2}^{4}\rangle}{\langle v_{2}^{2}\rangle^{2}}, \tag{3.83}\] \[\Big{(}\frac{v_{n}\{4\}}{v_{n}\{2\}}\Big{)}^{4} = 1-\frac{\sigma^{2}(v_{n}^{2})}{\langle v_{2}^{2}\rangle^{2}}.\]

Therefore, the ratio \(v_{n}\{4\}/v_{n}\{2\}\) can be used to estimate the strength of the flow fluctuations as a fraction of the measured flow harmonic. Here, a large value of \(v_{n}\{4\}/v_{n}\{2\}\) indicates less fluctuations whereas a smaller value indicate large fluctuations.

### 3.4 Systematic uncertainty

The measurements reported in this dissertation, involve both statistical and systematic uncertainties. The statistical uncertainties are the outcome of randomly determined fluctuations stemming from the fact that each measurement reflects a finite set of observations. On the other hand, systematic uncertainties arise from uncertainties correlated with the nature of the measurement equipment, hypotheses made by the experimenter, and the model used to make conclusions based on the observed data. A conventional way to evaluate the magnitude of these systematic uncertainties, is to vary the sensitive cut-variables around their nominal cut-value and determine their effect on the final measurements. The width of the resulting distribution of the difference in the final measurements is quoted as the systematic uncertainty. In this section, I will discuss a representative set of these cut-variables (Tab.3.1) for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV.

The systematic uncertainties associated with the \(\rm{v_{n}}\{{k}\}\) (\(k\) = 2 and 4) measurements were estimated through studies of the influence of the choice of the cut-variables summarized in Tab. 3.1. Representative results for these cuts are summarized in Fig. 3.11. They indicate relatively small systematic uncertainties. For the \(\rm{v_{1}^{even}}\) measurements, another source of the systematic uncertainties associated with the fitting procedure was included. For the latter, the \(v_{11}\)\(NxM\) matrix dimension (see Eq. 3.24) was varied in order to study systematically the effect of the matrix size on the fit procedure. The influence of this variation is summarized in Fig. 3.12. The respective uncertainties from these studies, which depend on beam energy as well as collision system, were added in quadrature to obtain an overall systematic uncertainty estimate. This systematic uncertainty estimate will be displayed as a shaded band or open box around the reported data points.

