## Chapter 4 Beam Energy and Collision System Dependence of Flow Harmonics

Anisotropic flow is derived from an eccentricity-driven hydrodynamic expansion of the plasma created in the collision zone [83, 84, 85, 86, 87, 88] produced by the colliding ions. That is, the eccentricity \(\epsilon_{n}\), drives unequal pressure gradients in- and out of the event plane \(\Psi_{n}\), leading to an anisotropic expansion that results in the anisotropic emission of particles about this plane. Thus, anisotropic flow measurements are sensitive to the initial-state eccentricity, the equation of state (EOS) and the transport properties of the medium. Accordingly, such measurements are at the forefront of ongoing efforts to extract the \(T\) and \(\mu_{B}\) dependence of the transport coefficient \(\eta/s\), as well as to constrain the initial-state eccentricity spectrum.

In this chapter, the differential and \(p_{T}\)-integrated flow harmonics for the systems studied are presented and discussed.

### 4.1 Beam Energy Dependence of \(v_{n}\)

The \(v_{n}\) measurements from the RHIC BES can aid the study of \(\eta/s\) for the broad range of \(\mu_{B}\) and \(T\), produced in these collisions [89]. Currently, there is little, if any, experimental constraints for the \(\mu_{B}\) and \(T\) dependence of \(\eta/s\), especially for the lower beam energies i.e, low \(T\) and large \(\mu_{B}\).

Theory suggests that \(\eta/s\) depends on both \(\mu_{B}\) and \(T\), and could have a minimum in the vicinity of the critical point [91]. A recent study involving the theoretical calculation of \(v_{2}\) for different values of \(\eta/s\) at each beam energy [89], indicate agreement with the data (Fig.4.1 ) only for an increase in \(\eta/s\) as the beam energy is lowered. For these calculations \(\eta/s\) was assumed to be constant during the evolution of the fireball at each collision energy. Since smaller values of \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) result in larger values of \(\mu_{B}\) and lower temperatures, Fig.4.1 suggests that \(\eta/s\) could have a dependence on both \(\mu_{B}\) and \(T\). The new BES \(v_{n}\) measurements presented in the following are expected to provide better constraints for theoretical models and hence, lead to a better characterization of \(\frac{\eta}{s}(\mu_{B},T)\).

