#### The rapidity-even dipolar flow \(v_{1}^{even}\)

In the absence of fluctuations, the directed flow \(\mathrm{v_{1}}\) develops along the direction of the impact parameter [92] which is an odd function, \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{odd}}(\eta)=-\mathrm{v_{1}^{odd}}(-\eta)\), of pseudorapidity. However, initial-state fluctuations, acting in concert with hydrodynamic-like expansion, gives an additional rapidity-even, \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}(\eta)=\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}(-\eta)\), component [73, 80] resulting in the total:

\[\mathrm{v_{1}}(\eta)=\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}(\eta)+\mathrm{v_{1}^{odd}}(\eta). \tag{4.1}\]

The magnitude of \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{odd}}(\eta)\) can be made negligible via a symmetric pseudorapidity selection, to give a straightforward measurement of \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}(\eta)\).

The rapidity-even \(\mathrm{v_{1}}\)is proportional to the fluctuations-driven dipole asymmetry \(\varepsilon_{1}\) of the system [73, 80, 93]; \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}\propto\varepsilon_{1}\), where \(\varepsilon_{1}\equiv\left<|r^{3}e^{i\phi}|\right>/\left<r^{3}\right>\) and averaging is taken over the initial energy density after re-centering the coordinate system, i.e., \(\left<|re^{i\phi}|\right>=0\). Hydrodynamical model calculations [74] indicate that the magnitude of \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}\) is sensitive to \(\eta\)/s, albeit with less sensitivity than for the higher order harmonics, \(\mathrm{n}\geq 2\). It has not been experimentally established whether this sensitivity depends on the temperature T, baryon chemical potential \(\mu_{B}\) or both. Similarly it is has not been established whether this sensitivity could reflect the influence of a possible critical end point (CEP) in the phase diagram for nuclear matter [94]. Therefore, differential \(\mathrm{v_{1}^{even}}\) measurements that span a broad range of \(\sqrt{\mathrm{s_{{}_{NN}}}}\) (\(T\) and \(\mu_{B}\)), could potentially provide (i) unique supplemental constraints to discern between different initial-state models, (ii) aid precision extraction of \(\eta\)/\(s\) and study its possible dependence on T and \(\mu_{\mathrm{B}}\), and (iii) give insight on the CEP.

