#### An acoustic model for the viscous attenuation of flow

As discussed earlier anisotropic flow measurements are sensitive to initial conditions, the equation of state (EOS) and the transport properties of the medium. Accordingly, studding the anisotropic flow for collision systems (U+U, Cu+Au, Cu+Cu, and d+Au) at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\sim\) 200 GeV will help to understand the effect of the initial conditions on the bulk observables of the QGP. Also will give an important insight about \(\eta/s\) temperature dependence.

Theoretical investigations show that \(\rm{v_{n}}\propto\varepsilon_{n}\) for elliptic- and triangular flow (n = 2 and 3) [83, 84, 85, 86, 87, 88], with proportionality constant that depends on the system size and the specific shear viscosity \(\frac{\eta}{s}(T)\), of the created medium. This proportionality constant also known as viscous attenuation which can be understood within an acoustic model framework, akin to that for viscous relativistic hydrodynamics [108, 109, 110]:

\[\delta T_{\mu\nu}(n,t) = \exp{\left(-\beta k^{2}\right)}\delta T_{\mu\nu}(0), \tag{4.3}\] \[\beta \propto \frac{\eta}{s}\frac{1}{\rm{R}^{2}}\frac{t}{T} \tag{4.4}\]

where the energy-momentum tensor \(T_{\mu\nu}\) incorporates the dispersion relation for sound propagation [111]. The viscous coefficient \(\beta(\rm{T})\propto\frac{\eta}{s}\), \(t\propto\rm{R}\) is the expansion time, T is the temperature, \(k=n/\rm{R}\) is the wave number (_i.e._\(2\pi\rm{R}=n\lambda\) for \(n\geq 1\)) and R is a characteristic geometric radius of the created medium.

Equation 4.3 suggests that for a given centrality, the viscous corrections to the flow harmonics \(v_{n}\) grow exponentially as \(n^{2}\):

\[\frac{v_{n}}{\varepsilon_{n}} \propto \exp{\left(-n^{2}\beta^{\prime}\frac{1}{RT}\right)}, \tag{4.5}\] \[\beta^{\prime} \propto \frac{\eta}{s}. \tag{4.6}\]

This leads to:

\[ln\left(\frac{v_{n}}{\varepsilon_{n}}\right)\propto-n^{2}\beta^{\prime}\frac{ 1}{RT}, \tag{4.7}\]

From entropy considerations, the dimensionless size RT, can be expressed in terms of the mean charged particle multiplicity density \(\langle\rm{N_{ch}}\rangle\) for a given centrality selection; \((\rm{RT})^{3}\propto\rm{dN_{ch}}/\rm{d}\eta\)[112]. Therefore:

\[\ln\left(\frac{v_{n}}{\varepsilon_{n}}\right)\propto-n^{2}\beta^{\prime} \langle N_{ch}\rangle^{-1/3}. \tag{4.8}\]

Consequently, Eq. 4.8 provides a transparent and consistent approach to test and compare the predicted dependence of \(\rm{v_{n}}\) on \(\varepsilon_{n}\), RT and \(\frac{\eta}{s}\) in a given collision system - small or large [113, 114].

Equation 4.8 suggests that at a given multiplicity, the magnitude of \(v_{n}\) for different systems, will be controlled by \(\epsilon_{n}\) and \(\eta/s\). For different collision systems the geometrical-driven eccentricity \(\epsilon_{2}\), is found to be largely dependent on the colliding systems. However the fluctuations-driven eccentricities \(\varepsilon_{1}\) and \(\epsilon_{3}\), are found to be very similar for different collision systems [115]. In the following, the measurements for \(v_{1}^{even}\) and \(v_{3}\) are discussed separately from those for \(v_{2}\) to highlight this distinction.

